PantryPulse – Comprehensive Documentation

=== Project Overview & Complete Project Analysis ===

Purpose: Real‑time IoT dashboard for commercial‑kitchen & food‑storage monitoring (weight, temperature, humidity, VOC, RFID, alerts).
Problem: Food waste & spoilage caused by lack of visibility into storage conditions and inventory.
Solution: ESP32 devices stream sensor data → Backend stores telemetry & generates alerts → Web UI visualises KPIs, shelf status, inventory, and alerts.
Target Users: Kitchen managers, hotel F&B directors, restaurant owners, facility operators, investors / demo reviewers.
Core Use‑Cases: View live KPI dashboard, inspect per‑shelf weight & environment, add / edit inventory items, receive & resolve alerts, configure thresholds, run RFID scans, export telemetry reports.
Key Features Implemented: Telemetry ingestion, realtime via Socket.IO, KPI dashboard, shelves grid, inventory table, alerts list, settings page, demo‑mode seed data.
Tech Stack: Frontend – React 18, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Lucide‑react, Recharts, Framer‑Motion, React‑Query. Backend – Node 18, Express, TypeScript, Socket.IO, Prisma 5 (SQLite), Zod.
Constraints: SQLite limits concurrent writes; no authentication; ESP32 must send valid JSON.

=== System Architecture & Data Flow ===

Frontend:
- Framework: React 18 (function components, hooks).
- Bundler: Vite (port 5173/5174).
- Routing: react‑router‑dom (AppRoutes defines /, /shelves, /inventory, /alerts, /settings).
- State: React‑Query for server state; local React state for UI toggles.
- Services: Central API wrapper src/lib/api.ts (placeholder methods).
- Realtime: socket.io-client (not yet imported) expects events telemetry:update, device:update, alert:update, etc.
- No authentication; CORS whitelist.
- UI: Sidebar, Layout, KPI cards, shelves grid, inventory table, alerts list, settings form.
- Responsive: Sidebar hidden on < md; no mobile drawer.

Backend:
- Framework: Express 4.x with TypeScript.
- Middleware: cors (whitelist), helmet, express.json, morgan (logging), custom errorHandler.
- Routes (mounted under /api): health, dashboard, telemetry, shelves, inventory, alerts, devices, settings, surplus, reports, rfid.
- Controllers: telemetryController handles validation, storage, alerts, realtime emit.
- Services: prisma (PrismaClient), http (helpers asyncRoute, HttpError, parseInput, parseRange).
- Realtime: Socket.IO server emits telemetry:update, device:update, alert:update, shelf:update, inventory:update, surplus:update, settings:update.
- Validation: Zod schemas for request bodies.
- No auth/authorisation; all endpoints open.
- DB: SQLite via Prisma schema (Device, Shelf, Telemetry, FoodItem, Batch, InventoryItem, Alert, SurplusItem, Setting).

Data Flow (Typical):
1. ESP32 sends POST /api/telemetry → validation → DB transaction → update Device.lastSeen → generate alerts → emit Socket.IO events.
2. Dashboard GET /api/dashboard → aggregated queries → UI renders KPI cards.
3. Shelf CRUD via POST/PATCH/DELETE routes → DB ops → socket events.
4. Inventory CRUD → auto-status calculation, surplus detection, socket events.
5. Alert management (read, resolve) → DB update → socket events.
6. Settings get/put → DB upsert → socket broadcast.
7. RFID scan → lookup batch/inventory.
8. Report export → CSV streaming.

=== Software Requirements Specification (SRS) ===

Functional Requirements (selected):
- FR‑001 Telemetry Ingestion (POST /api/telemetry) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑002 Dashboard Data Retrieval (GET /api/dashboard) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑003 Shelf CRUD (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE /api/shelves) – IMPLEMENTED (Delete blocked if referenced).
- FR‑004 Inventory CRUD + Auto‑Status (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE /api/inventory) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑005 Alert Management (GET /api/alerts, PATCH read/resolve) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑006 Settings CRUD (GET /api/settings, PUT /api/settings/:key) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑007 RFID Scan (POST /api/rfid/scan) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑008 Report Export (GET /api/reports/export.csv) – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑009 Device CRUD – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑010 Surplus Detection & Action – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑011 Demo Mode Seed – IMPLEMENTED.
- FR‑012 Real‑time Updates – IMPLEMENTED (events emitted).

Non‑Functional Requirements:
- Performance: Telemetry ingest ≤ 30 ms; dashboard aggregation ≤ 500 ms.
- Scalability: SQLite limited – replace with PostgreSQL for production.
- Security: No auth (major gap); Helmet adds basic headers; CORS whitelist.
- Reliability: Server restarts preserve DB; no retry logic for telemetry.
- Usability: UI minimal, lacks loading skeletons, mobile drawer, dark‑mode toggle.
- Accessibility: No ARIA attributes, focus management – needs work.
- Data Retention: No purge – DB grows indefinitely.
- Logging: morgan logs requests; console errors – no structured logging.

Gaps:
- Authentication & role‑based permissions – MISSING.
- Mobile‑first navigation – MISSING.
- Dark‑mode toggle – MISSING.
- Rate‑limiting – MISSING.
- Unit / integration tests – MISSING.
- OpenAPI spec – MISSING.
- Pagination on list endpoints – MISSING.
- Structured logging, health endpoint for Socket.IO – MISSING.

=== API & Database Documentation ===

API Endpoints (summary):
- GET /api/health – health check.
- GET /api/dashboard?range=... – aggregated metrics.
- POST /api/telemetry – ingest telemetry (validation via Zod, creates alerts, emits events).
- GET /api/shelves – list shelves with telemetry, device, sensor list, inventory count.
- GET /api/shelves/:shelfId – detailed shelf view (not used by UI yet).
- POST /api/shelves – create shelf (validate with createShelfSchema).
- PATCH /api/shelves/:shelfId – update shelf.
- DELETE /api/shelves/:shelfId – delete if no references.
- GET /api/inventory – searchable list (filters: search, shelfId, category).
- POST /api/inventory – add inventory (creates food, batch if missing).
- PATCH /api/inventory/:id – update inventory.
- DELETE /api/inventory/:id – cascade delete batch/food if empty.
- GET /api/alerts – list alerts (filters: severity, resolved, unread).
- PATCH /api/alerts/:id/read – mark read/unread.
- POST /api/alerts/read-all – bulk mark read.
- PATCH /api/alerts/:id/resolve – resolve alert.
- GET /api/devices – list devices.
- POST /api/devices – register device.
- PATCH /api/devices/:deviceId – update device.
- DELETE /api/devices/:deviceId – delete if no references.
- GET /api/settings – retrieve merged defaults + DB overrides.
- PUT /api/settings/:key – upsert setting (editable keys only).
- GET /api/surplus – list surplus items.
- POST /api/surplus/:inventoryId – mark inventory as surplus.
- PATCH /api/surplus/:id/action – set surplus action (DONATION, USED, DISMISSED).
- POST /api/rfid/scan – lookup batch/inventory by RFID.
- GET /api/reports – JSON aggregated report.
- GET /api/reports/export.csv – CSV telemetry export.

All endpoints return JSON; proper error handling via HttpError; validation errors result in 400.

Database Schema (Prisma):
- Device (id PK, deviceId unique string, name, ip, status, lastSeen) – one‑to‑many Shelves, Telemetry.
- Shelf (id PK, shelfId unique string, name, maxCapacity Float?, deviceId FK) – many Telemetry, InventoryItem, Sensors.
- Telemetry (id PK, deviceId String FK to Device.deviceId, shelfId String FK to Shelf.shelfId, weight, temperature, humidity, voc, gasStatus, timestamp).
- FoodItem (id PK, name, category) – one‑to‑many Batches.
- Batch (id PK, batchId unique, foodItemId FK, quantity, weightKg, expiryDate, rfidTagId?, shelfId optional) – one‑to‑many InventoryItem.
- InventoryItem (id PK, batchId FK, shelfId optional FK, quantity, weight, addedAt, expiryDate, status enum) – optional SurplusItem.
- Alert (id PK, type enum, severity enum, description, shelfId?, deviceId?, createdAt, read bool, resolved bool).
- SurplusItem (id PK, inventoryId FK, action nullable enum, detectedAt).
- Setting (id PK, key unique, value string).

Key notes:
- Telemetry foreign keys are stored as strings (deviceId, shelfId) but controller currently writes numeric IDs – type mismatch to be fixed.
- Duplicate column maxCapacity vs capacityKg – capacityKg unused.
- Indexes only on PK/unique; consider index on Telemetry.timestamp for large data.

=== Code‑Quality & Architecture Audit ===

Major Issues:
1. Mixed naming (maxCapacity vs capacityKg) – remove unused column.
2. Telemetry FK type mismatch – change to Int.
3. Monolithic route files – split into controllers.
4. No authentication – critical security gap.
5. No pagination on heavy list endpoints – scalability risk.
6. No rate‑limiting – potential DoS.
7. No unit/integration tests – high regression risk.
8. No structured logging – difficult monitoring.
9. No Socket.IO client integration – realtime UI not functional.
10. Missing CRUD forms in UI – incomplete user experience.

Recommendations (priority):
- P0: Implement JWT auth, protect mutating routes.
- P0: Fix Telemetry FK types.
- P1: Add Socket.IO client to React, set up listeners.
- P1: Build CRUD forms for Shelf, Device, Inventory with react‑hook‑form + Zod.
- P1: Add rate‑limiting middleware on telemetry.
- P2: Add pagination to inventory & dashboard telemetry.
- P2: Add DB indexes on Telemetry.timestamp.
- P2: Implement dark‑mode toggle with persistence.
- P3: Write unit & integration tests (Jest, React Testing Library).
- P3: Generate OpenAPI (Swagger) spec.
- P3: Add CI/CD workflow.
- P3: Switch to PostgreSQL for production readiness.

=== Audit Summary & Roadmap ===
Current State:
- Telemetry ingestion works, generates alerts, emits events.
- Dashboard UI displays KPI cards (static data) and chart placeholder.
- Shelves list and inventory table render data.
- Alerts list works; resolve button works.
- Settings UI shows key/value but lacks validation feedback.
- Real‑time events emitted server‑side; client not listening.
- No authentication, no pagination, no rate‑limiting, no tests.

Major Problems:
1. No authentication/authorisation.
2. Telemetry FK type mismatch.
3. Frontend lacks real‑time integration & CRUD forms.
4. Scalability limited by SQLite & missing pagination.
5. Missing testing & CI.

Recommended short‑term (first 2 weeks) plan:
Day 1‑2: Implement JWT auth middleware; protect routes.
Day 3‑4: Fix Telemetry FK types (schema + controller) and run migration.
Day 5: Add Socket.IO client integration in React (connect, listeners, cache updates).
Day 6‑7: Build CRUD forms for Shelf, Device, Inventory with validation.
Day 8: Add rate‑limiting middleware on telemetry.
Day 9: Add pagination to inventory & dashboard telemetry endpoints.
Day 10: Add indexes on Telemetry.timestamp.
Day 11‑12: Write unit tests for telemetry handling and alerts.
Day 13: Integrate structured logging (winston) and replace console logs.
Day 14: Clean dead imports, remove duplicate columns, ensure naming consistency.

After this sprint the project will have secure APIs, accurate DB schema, real‑time UI updates, basic forms, and a foundation for testing and production deployment.

